# Shared Bazel defaults, deposited by chezmoi on every machine.
# Cap locally executing build actions at half the machine's logical CPU
# cores, minus one, so that even two concurrent builds always leave at
# least a couple of cores free for the system itself.
#
# Bazel's --local_resources value grammar takes HOST_CPUS with at most one
# operator ("*<float>" or "-<float>"), so "half minus one" cannot be written
# as HOST_CPUS*.5-1 (the older dedicated --local_cpu_resources spelling was
# removed in Bazel 9 anyway). This template therefore computes the number at
# apply time: getconf(1) reports online logical CPUs on both Linux and macOS
# (GNU nproc is absent from stock macOS). Integer division by 2, minus one,
# clamped at 1 so machines with fewer than four cores still schedule local
# work. The value adapts per machine at each chezmoi apply; re-apply after a
# machine's CPU count changes.
#
# A workspace .bazelrc or the command line still overrides this per project.
# Remote-execution users on current Bazel may also want: --jobs=HOST_CPUS*.5
{{- $cores := int (trim (output "getconf" "_NPROCESSORS_ONLN")) }}
build --local_resources=cpu={{ max 1 (sub (div $cores 2) 1) }}

# Remote cache: BuildBarn on broker (Tailscale, grpc plaintext). Gated to
# Linux hosts via platform-specific config, so macOS machines sharing this
# file skip it entirely. Per-workspace user.bazelrc and the command line
# still override these for experiments.
common --enable_platform_specific_config
build:linux --remote_cache=grpc://broker:8980
build:linux --remote_upload_local_results=true
build:linux --remote_download_outputs=toplevel
